  nonce: cdae  # cdae
  data:  # cdae
    kind: invariant  # cdae
    title: "swift: Alamofire's open class Session (Source/Core/Session.swift) is not extracted -- its methods surface as top-level functions and the class name is treated as an external type"  # cdae
    status: violated  # cdae
    priority: 3  # cdae
    parent:  # cdae
    tags:  # cdae
    - swift  # cdae
    - analyzer  # cdae
    - analysis_quality  # cdae
    isbefore: []  # cdae
    duplicate_of: []  # cdae
    not_duplicate_of: []  # cdae
    pr_ref:  # cdae
    description: "Observed 2026-09-04 while measuring INV-kotob on Alamofire: `open class Session: @unchecked Sendable {` (Source/Core/Session.swift, 1457 lines) is NOT among the extracted symbols — its methods (`webSocketRequest`, `withAllRequests`, `cancelAllRequests`, ...) are extracted as top-level FUNCTIONS instead (82 symbols from the file, no class). A three-line snippet `open class Session: @unchecked Sendable { func go() {} }` extracts `Session` as a class, so the miss is file-specific: something earlier in the file drops the class_declaration (an ERROR node from newer Swift syntax is the first hypothesis; not confirmed). Consequences: every `Session.x` / `session.x` receiver in Alamofire is treated as an EXTERNAL type (`swift:Session:0-0:request:unresolved` reached the module slot in the kotob arm B), and the class's 100+ methods are mis-kinded. Repro: `analyze_swift` on a dir holding only that file; check for a `class` symbol named `Session`. Lab: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/higob_swiftobjc_09032026/README.md (pass 1 read-back)."  # cdae
    fields:  # cdae
      statement: "Every class_declaration in a parsed Swift file MUST yield a class symbol, and its methods MUST be kinded as methods of that class, never as top-level functions."  # cdae
      root_cause: "UNCONFIRMED and file-specific: a three-line snippet with the same header extracts a class; Source/Core/Session.swift (1457 lines) yields 82 symbols and no class. First hypothesis: an ERROR node from newer Swift syntax earlier in the file swallows the class_declaration."  # cdae
- op: discuss  # ce45
  at: '2026-09-04T07:24:47Z'  # ce45
  by: agent  # ce45
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ce45
  clock: 2  # ce45
  nonce: ce45  # ce45
  message: "ROOT CAUSE FOUND 2026-09-04 (bisected Source/Core/Session.swift by prefix, first erroring prefix ends at line 509): a conditional-compilation block inside the class body -- '#if canImport(Darwin) && !canImport(FoundationNetworking)' wrapping 'open func webSocketRequest' -- is not parsed by tree-sitter-swift; error recovery re-parents the class header ('open class Session: @unchecked Sendable {' becomes simple_identifier + ERROR spanning lines 30-511) and the class's members surface under ERROR nodes, so no class symbol is minted and its methods are kinded as top-level functions. A 3-line class with the same header parses clean; the '#if' inside the body is the trigger. Consequences beyond symbol extraction: the receiver-type scoping in swift.py treats a declaration under an ERROR as file-level and walks lookups through ERRORs (PR for the registry consumer), which keeps 'rootQueue.async' typed in this file, but sites whose declarations are re-parented arbitrarily still lose (part of the 105 residual losses on Alamofire). Fix shape: pre-strip '#if/#else/#endif' directive lines inside type bodies before parsing (keeping line numbers by replacing them with blank lines), or teach the grammar; the former is local and testable."  # ce45
- op: discuss  # deb3
  at: '2026-09-04T08:12:06Z'  # deb3
  by: agent  # deb3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # deb3
  clock: 3  # deb3
  nonce: deb3  # deb3
  message: "SIZED 2026-09-04 (tree-sitter-swift as installed; probe: parse every .swift, count files with ERROR nodes). Alamofire: 15 of 96 files parse with ERROR nodes (111 nodes), 13 of them have an indented `#if` — the Session.swift trigger. VernissageServer: 263 of 906 files (29%) parse with ERROR nodes — 10,366 nodes — and NONE has an indented `#if`: 225 of the 263 are Swift Testing files whose first error sits at `extension ControllersTests {` (bodies of `@Test(...) func ...` / `@Suite(...)` macro attributes), 16 start at `@Suite(\"...\")`, the rest at `if try await ...` / `if let x: T = try? await ...` / backticked function names. So the invariant is broader than `#if`: tree-sitter-swift's grammar lags Swift 5.9+ (macros, `@Suite`/`@Test`, some expression forms), and error recovery re-parents whole type bodies, which is why the receiver-type scoping had to grow an ERROR rule (PR #760). Fix shape, in order of payoff: (1) upgrade the tree-sitter-swift grammar and re-measure the file counts; (2) pre-strip `#if/#else/#endif` directive lines inside type bodies (line-preserving) for the `#if` family; (3) treat a `@Test`/`@Suite`-attributed declaration like any other (grammar work). The counts are the acceptance test: 15/96 and 263/906 must fall."  # deb3
- op: discuss  # 3fe8
  at: '2026-09-04T08:45:47Z'  # 3fe8
  by: agent  # 3fe8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3fe8
  clock: 4  # 3fe8
  nonce: 3fe8  # 3fe8
  message: "Grammar bumped in PR #763 (dev 435d284fc5): tree-sitter-swift 0.0.1 → 0.7.3 (packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/pyproject.toml). Behavioural evidence on the filed repro: `analyze_swift` on Alamofire now mints `Session` as a class (was absent) — regression test test_swift_conditional_compilation.py fails under 0.0.1 and passes under 0.7.3 (a `#if` block inside a class body; the class symbol, its methods kinded as methods, `rootQueue.async` typed). Corpus numbers: Alamofire ERROR files 15/96 → 5 (nodes 111 → 15); classes 181 → 184 (Session, Request present), methods 1687 → 1857; io chains 31 → 30 (2 re-keyed onto their real methods, 1 false attribution removed, 0 lost); Vernissage unchanged (263 ERROR files, io 8 → 8). What this does NOT close: VernissageServer's 263 error-recovered files are Swift Testing (`@Suite` / `@Test` macro) files that 0.7.3 still cannot parse (10,364 ERROR nodes, unchanged) — the invariant stays VIOLATED for that family; the receiver-type scoping's ERROR rule (#760) keeps their receivers usable. Next step for that half is upstream grammar work or a pre-parse rewrite of macro attributes; both need their own sizing."  # 3fe8
- op: discuss  # df42
  at: '2026-09-04T11:22:15Z'  # df42
  by: agent  # df42
  actor: jgstern_agent  # df42
  clock: 5  # df42
  nonce: df42  # df42
  message: "THE ROOT CAUSE THIS ITEM RECORDED FOR ITS RESIDUAL HALF IS REFUTED, and the half is now\nclosed on the corrected one.\n\nThe 08-12 sizing entry named the `@Suite` / `@Test` macro attributes, on the evidence that\n225 of VernissageServer's 263 error-parsing files are Swift Testing files whose first ERROR\nnode starts at `extension ControllersTests {`. Bisected the constructs one at a time under\nthe shipped 0.7.3 grammar: `@Suite(\"T\", .serialized, .tags(.x))` on a struct, `@Test`,\n`@Test(\"desc\", .tags(.x))` and `#expect(a == b)` ALL parse with ZERO ERROR nodes. The\nreported line is where error recovery re-parented TO, not where the failure is; reading it\nas the cause is what produced the wrong root cause. The real triggers are (a) a backtick\nRAW IDENTIFIER containing spaces -- Swift 6.1's spelling for a test name, `` func `hashtags\nare returned for an anonymous user`() `` -- and (b) `try` inside an `if` / `guard` / `while`\ncondition.\n\nSHIPPED: a byte-length-preserving pre-parse rewrite of exactly those two spellings, behind a\nnew `TreeSitterAnalyzer.parse_source` template method (default = the single parse every\nanalyzer already did). Guarded twice: it runs ONLY on a file that already fails to parse, and\nits result is kept ONLY when it strictly reduces ERROR nodes -- so a parseable file is\nbyte-identical through the hook, which is what stops a backtick or a `try` inside a STRING\nliteral from being rewritten. Byte length is preserved so every span still points at real\nsource, and the returned tree is the tree of the returned bytes so Pass 1 and Pass 2 cannot\ndesynchronise.\n\nTHIS ITEM'S OWN ACCEPTANCE TEST (\"the counts are the acceptance test: 15/96 and 263/906 must\nfall\"), measured:\n    VernissageServer   error files 263/906 -> 5    ERROR nodes 10,364 -> 19\n    Alamofire          error files   5/96  -> 5    ERROR nodes      15 -> 15\nAlamofire's five are the OTHER family (`#if` in a position 0.7.3 still mishandles) and the\nrewrite correctly leaves them alone. The 5 Vernissage residuals: `if`-conditions written across\nCONTINUED lines, `switch try await`, three identifiers containing a literal U+200B\nZERO-WIDTH SPACE (in Vernissage's own source), and one `#if os(Linux)`. Widening the\nline scope to `switch` was MEASURED rather than assumed and fixes zero further files, so\nit stays narrow.\n\nTHROUGH THE ANALYZER, VernissageServer: symbols 7,373 -> 7,996, methods 1,802 -> 2,901,\ncall edges 14,139 -> 21,484; Alamofire byte-identical on every count (the control);\nio-boundaries unchanged on both. Per call SITE on Vernissage 12,544 -> 19,651 sites,\n7,853 changed, 4,917 ABSENT -> resolved, and the rig's 375 \"losses\" were ALL read back\nand NONE is real: 365 SRC re-keys (a recovered file stops emitting top-level functions\nand starts emitting methods, so the site key moves), 4 error-recovery mis-attributions\nremoved (arm A put a call on a line whose source contains no such call), and 6 FALSE\nresolutions withdrawn -- arm A had minted seven spurious BARE top-level `variable`\nsymbols named settings/categories/users/message out of error-recovered test bodies, and\nswift's bare-name lookup bound to them. My first explanation for those 6 (new symbols\nmaking the name ambiguous) was REFUTED by the data: arm B has FEWER same-short-name\nsymbols despite 623 more overall.\n\nCONSEQUENCE WORTH RECORDING: after the rewrite, ZERO files in either repository parent a\n`property_declaration` under an ERROR node -- the only input that reaches the\n`through_errors=False` break in `_function_ancestors`, which PR #760 added precisely because\nof these files. The rule is not dead (it guards grammar gaps the rewrite does not cover) but\nits corpus population is now zero, so its test fixture was deliberately re-based onto\n`switch try await`, which sits outside the rewrite's `if`/`guard`/`while` line scope by\ndesign and therefore still errors.\n\nLab: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/bisok_swifttesting_09042026/ (README carries the bisect table,\nthe sizing script and the fixture-choice reasoning).\n\nSTATUS STAYS violated, DELIBERATELY. The item's statement is universal (\"EVERY\nclass_declaration ... MUST yield a class symbol\"), and 10 files across the two repositories\nstill violate it: Alamofire's 5 (`#if` in a position 0.7.3 mishandles) and Vernissage's 5\n(`if`-conditions written across continued lines, `switch try await`, three identifiers\ncontaining a literal U+200B, one `#if os(Linux)`). Marking it satisfied on the strength of\n263 -> 5 would be exactly the proxy-for-behaviour closure the closure-evidence discipline\nnames. What HAS changed is that the remaining population is now enumerated rather than\nestimated, and the family the item's residual half named -- Swift Testing -- is closed.\n\nNo separate residual item is filed: this item IS the tracker for this invariant, and its\nopen population is now precisely known, so a second item for the same statement would be\nnoise rather than precision."  # df42
